---
import BlogPostLayout from '../../../layouts/BlogPostLayout.astro';
import { loadAllSnapshots, loadManifest, loadClassSnapshot } from '@data/loadSnapshot';
import {
  EVIDENCE_DEFINITIONS,
  formatDate,
  stageLabel,
} from '@data/derive';
import { CC_BY_4_0_URL, TALENTSFOREVER_URL } from '@data/credit';

const manifest = loadManifest();
const snapshots = loadAllSnapshots();
const source = manifest.sources[0];

const statusCounts = new Map<string, number>();
for (const s of snapshots) {
  for (const t of s.talents) {
    for (const r of t.rankEffects) {
      statusCounts.set(
        r.evidenceStatus,
        (statusCounts.get(r.evidenceStatus) ?? 0) + 1,
      );
    }
  }
}
const statusRows = EVIDENCE_DEFINITIONS.map((d) => ({
  ...d,
  count: statusCounts.get(d.status) ?? 0,
}));
const totalRanks = manifest.coverage.ranksTotal;

// A concrete example of a rank text next to its Classic counterpart.
const druid = loadClassSnapshot('druid');
const example = druid.talents.find((t) => t.talentId === 'druid:nature-s-reach');
const exampleRank1 = example?.rankEffects.find((r) => r.rank === 1);
---

<BlogPostLayout slug="where-wow-forever-talent-data-comes-from">
  <p>
    Every effect description on this site, from a one-line passive to a
    cooldown, came from somewhere, and the honest answer to "how accurate is
    it?" depends on where. This post lays out the chain: who produces the
    data, how it reaches the calculator, what the labels attached to each
    rank mean, and the specific ways the numbers can be out of date or
    wrong. It is the longer version of the <a href="/sources/">Sources page</a>,
    written for someone deciding how much weight to put on a talent
    description.
  </p>

  <h2>The source</h2>
  <p>
    The talent data comes from
    <a href={TALENTSFOREVER_URL} rel="external">talentsforever.com</a>, a
    separate fan-made WoW Forever talent calculator that publishes its data
    export under the
    <a href={CC_BY_4_0_URL} rel="external">Creative Commons Attribution 4.0</a>
    license. That license lets anyone reuse and adapt the data as long as
    they credit the source and say if they changed it. We credit it in the
    calculator, on the Sources page and here, and the changes we make are
    described below.
  </p>
  <p>
    According to the notes shipped with that export, the talents are read
    from the WoW Forever beta client's own data files, and they describe the
    upstream site as having begun with frame-by-frame reads of the BlizzCon
    2026 demo. The same notes add two caveats worth repeating. The
    numbers "can lag live tuning", meaning the client files may not reflect
    server-side changes. And a class can carry server hotfixes on top of the
    build it was read from, in which case the upstream data labels that class
    and names the days.
  </p>
  <p>
    The current snapshot was recorded on {formatDate(source.recordedAt)}
    {
      manifest.gameBuild ? (
        <> from beta build {manifest.gameBuild}</>
      ) : null
    }
    and published on {formatDate(manifest.publishedAt)}. Its stage is
    marked "{stageLabel(manifest.stage)}", which is a deliberate reminder
    that this is pre-release data.
  </p>

  <h2>What happens between the export and the page</h2>
  <p>
    We keep the raw export file in our repository and run an import script
    that turns it into one JSON file per class plus a manifest. Three things
    happen in that step:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>
      Each talent gets a stable ID that does not depend on its grid position,
      so a talent that moves rows is still the same talent for share links.
    </li>
    <li>
      The file names include a content hash and old snapshot IDs stay on the
      manifest, which is what lets older share links keep opening.
    </li>
    <li>
      Each rank gets an evidence status, and every prerequisite is resolved
      to a talent ID. The manifest counts how many resolved, which is how we
      notice a prerequisite that points at nothing.
    </li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    We reformat and annotate, but we do not rewrite effect text by hand. If a
    description reads oddly, it reads that way in the source.
  </p>

  <h2>Reading the evidence labels</h2>
  <p>
    Every rank carries one of seven statuses. They are ordered roughly from
    strongest claim to weakest, and the Sources page defines them in full:
  </p>
  <div class="table-scroll">
    <table class="data-table">
      <caption class="visually-hidden">
        Evidence statuses and how many ranks currently carry each
      </caption>
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th scope="col">Status</th>
          <th scope="col">What it claims</th>
          <th scope="col">Ranks now</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        {
          statusRows.map((r) => (
            <tr>
              <td>
                <span class={`badge badge-${r.status}`}>{r.label}</span>
              </td>
              <td>{r.definition}</td>
              <td>{r.count}</td>
            </tr>
          ))
        }
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
  <p>
    Out of {totalRanks} ranks in the current snapshot, the counts above show
    where they sit. The number to focus on is how many ranks carry one of the
    "verified" or "official" statuses, because those are the only labels that
    mean we, or Blizzard, confirmed something directly. A "community record"
    is the label for text relayed from the source export that we have not
    checked against the client ourselves. It is not a statement that the
    text is wrong. It is a statement about who vouches for it, and the
    answer is the upstream project, not us.
  </p>
  <p>
    That is also why the calculator never presents a rank text as simply
    "confirmed". The detail panel prints the label right under the text, and
    a rank with no known effect shows a question mark in the tree instead of
    an invented description.
  </p>

  {
    example && exampleRank1 && example.classic?.text ? (
      <>
        <h2>An example: one talent, two eras</h2>
        <p>
          Here is {example.name} in the Druid Balance tree, as the current
          data records it next to the Classic version it is compared with.
        </p>
        <blockquote>
          <p>
            <strong>WoW Forever, rank 1:</strong> {exampleRank1.text}
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Classic reference:</strong> {example.classic.text}
          </p>
        </blockquote>
        <p>
          The talent's status against Classic is "{example.classic.status}".
          The difference matters for planning, and it is exactly the kind of
          thing worth confirming in game if a decision depends on it. See the{' '}
          <a href="/talents/druid/">Druid reference page</a> for every rank
          and its label.
        </p>
      </>
    ) : null
  }

  <h2>Ways the data can be wrong or stale</h2>
  <ol>
    <li>
      <strong>A newer beta build changed a talent.</strong> We update the
      snapshot when a new build is available in the upstream data, so there
      can be a gap. Class pages show the build and date of the data in use,
      and the <a href="/changelog/">changelog</a> lists every update by class.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Hotfixes.</strong> Server-side changes may not be in the client
      files at all. If a talent behaves differently in game than the text
      says, that is the first thing to suspect.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Level and gear effects.</strong> The source gives tooltip text as
      the client files have it at level 60. Values that scale can read
      differently on your character.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Transcription or import errors.</strong> A typo, a wrong
      prerequisite or a misplaced row is possible in any pipeline.
      The manifest shows that {manifest.coverage.prerequisitesResolved} of
      {' '}{manifest.coverage.prerequisitesTotal} recorded prerequisites
      resolved to a talent, but "resolved" means the link exists, not that it
      is right.
    </li>
  </ol>

  <h2>What we ask readers to do</h2>
  <p>
    Treat the site as a planning aid and not as an authority. For any pick
    that matters, open the talent in the game's own talent window and compare
    the text. When the two disagree, send us the class, the talent name, the
    rank, the build number and a screenshot through the
    <a href="/contact/">contact page</a>, or open an issue on the project's
    GitHub repository. Reports that include the snapshot ID shown on the
    Sources page are the easiest to check, because they tell us exactly
    which data you were looking at.
  </p>
  <p>
    The game, its names, talent text and icons belong to Blizzard
    Entertainment, which has no connection to this site. The CC BY 4.0
    license applies to the upstream data export and does not change that. The
    <a href="/about/">About page</a> has the full independence notice, and the
    post on <a href="/blog/how-to-use-the-wow-forever-talent-calculator/">using the calculator</a>
    shows where each label appears in the interface.
  </p>
</BlogPostLayout>
